.TH OPEN 2
.SH NAME
open, create, close, mkdir \- open, create or make a file
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B import "vsys:libuser"
.PP
.nf
.B "open :: proc(path: string, flags: u64) -> i64"
.B "create :: proc(path: string, flags: u64, mode: u64) -> i64"
.B "close :: proc(fd: int) -> i64"
.B "mkdir :: proc(path: string, mode: u64 = 0o777) -> i64"
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Open
resolves
.I path
in the caller's own namespace and answers a descriptor, the lowest number
not already in use.
.I Flags
is one of
.B abi.O_RDONLY
(0),
.B O_WRONLY
(1) or
.B O_RDWR
(2), with
.B O_TRUNC
(0o1000) added to truncate the file to zero length as it opens.
Unlike Plan 9, there is no
.BR OEXEC ,
.B OCEXEC
or
.BR ORCLOSE :
a program executes a file with
.I spawn
or
.I exec
(see
.IR fork (2)
and
.IR exec (2)),
and every descriptor a program holds is closed when it exits, without
asking.
.PP
.I Create
makes a new file at
.I path
or truncates an existing one, opens it as
.I open
would with
.IR flags ,
and answers a descriptor on it.
.I Mode
carries the permission bits described in
.IR stat (2).
If
.B DMDIR
is set in
.IR mode ,
the kernel makes a directory instead of a plain file, and the descriptor
.I create
answers is open on the new directory.
.PP
.I Mkdir
is
.I create
with
.B O_RDONLY
and
.B DMDIR
set in the mode:
.IP
.EX
mkdir(path, mode) == create(path, O_RDONLY, mode | DMDIR)
.EE
.PP
.I Close
closes the descriptor.
.SH SOURCE
.B sys/libuser/sys.odin
.br
.B kernel/user/syscall.odin
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR intro (2),
.IR bind (2),
.IR read (2),
.IR remove (2),
.IR stat (2)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Each call answers a non-negative descriptor on success and a negative
.B \-errno
on failure; see
.IR errstr (2).
.I Close
answers
.B EBADF
for a descriptor not open, unlike Plan 9's
.IR close ,
which never fails.
